## GPU Memory Budget

At idle (server running, EasyOCR models loaded), the Python process uses ~10.9 GiB of the 24.6 GiB VRAM. This leaves ~13.7 GiB available for inference workloads during job processing.

## Observed Workload Memory Profile

Measured 2026-05-20 via `DevEnv/capacity_test.py` with 4 concurrent jobs processing
a 10-page architectural PDF (`derekfirst.pdf`, 2592x1728 pts, AutoCAD output):

| Metric | Peak | Average |
|---|---|---|
| **System RAM used (`free -h`)** | **10.18 GiB** / 44 GiB (23%) | -- |
| **GPU VRAM used (`nvidia-smi`)** | **12.73 GiB** / 24 GiB (52%) | -- |
| Per-worker RSS | 2.76-2.80 GiB | 1.42 GiB |
| Parent process RSS | 0.51 GiB | -- |
| Sum of Python RSS (overcounts shared pages) | 11.10 GiB | -- |

Implications:

- **Production server is heavily over-provisioned on RAM** (peak ~23% of 44 GiB).
  Anything with >=24 GiB RAM has ample headroom for `MAX_WORKERS=4`.
- **GPU VRAM is the binding constraint at 52% peak**. Any replacement GPU
  needs >=24 GiB VRAM to match (e.g. NVIDIA A10G in AWS g5 family is a near match).
- Per-worker RSS is bimodal: ~1.4 GiB steady-state between jobs (EasyOCR + TATR
  loaded) and bursts to ~2.8 GiB during page rendering / OCR inference.

## Constraints and Limits

- **Single GPU**: Only one NVIDIA RTX A5000; no multi-GPU support
- **No swap**: Swap is disabled (`0B`); OOM kills are possible if RAM is exhausted
- **Four workers**: `MAX_WORKERS=4` in `AnvilUplinkCode/uplink_server.py` (line 44);
  workers are persistent subprocesses that each load EasyOCR + the v7 TATR model
  once at warmup and reuse them across jobs
- **Thread pinning**: `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS` set to 1 at startup for deterministic OCR
- **VRAM ceiling**: High-DPI rendering + EasyOCR + large PDFs can approach the 24 GiB limit

